/** @mainpage USBRadioPlus developer reference

USBRadioPlus implements the RadioPlus Asterisk channel technology, native radio
signaling, source processing, and the interactive tuning utility. This reference
describes implementation contracts. The installed manual pages describe radio
configuration and operation.

@section architecture Source layout

Transmit observability uses opt-in Asterisk debug level 5 records prefixed
URP_TXTRACE. Request, render, queue, write, and HID-call boundaries are distinct:
generation or enqueue is not DAC playback, and HID return is not RF confirmation.
OSS trace records report occupied bytes, including partial fragments. The
PortAudio trace reports submission results rather than a per-write playback
timestamp. Its playout guard separately uses the stream's output-latency
estimate obtained at open. See the configuration manual for trace enablement
and disable instructions. No new PTT policy or audio buffering is introduced
by the trace.

- chan_usbradioplus.c adapts OSS/libusb radio devices to Asterisk.
- chan_usbradioplus_modern.c adapts the ASL radio-device API and PortAudio.
- usbradioplus_channel_common.h defines shared channel lifecycle and tuning calls.
- usbradioplus_channel_core.h defines PCM routing, the lock-free program ring,
  and echo state.
- usbradioplus_native_tick.c directly renders local receive, final
  voice/telemetry processing, CTCSS/DCS, and DAC PCM in the native hardware
  callback.
- usbradioplus_processing.h exposes validated profiles and live processing controls.
- txagc_config describes the shared FFmpeg graph. Filtering, emphasis,
  equalization, dynamics, and limiting are implemented by that graph.
- usbradioplus_radio.h contains carrier, CTCSS, DCS, measurement, and
  transmit-signaling state.
- usbradioplus_ctcss.h, usbradioplus_dcs.h, and usbradioplus_hardware.h provide
  tone/code generation and parallel-port radio programming.
- scripts/usbradioplus-tune implements the screen-reader-friendly tuner.
- tests and tests_py contain executable unit, failure-injection, integration,
  configuration, utility, and install checks. Upstream reference fixtures are test data.

@section units Audio buffers and units

The CM119 path runs at 48,000 samples per second with 960 samples in a 20 ms
mono block. App_rpt starts at 8,000 samples per second; each channel stores its
rate explicitly. The program-ring converter uses a nominal one-to-one ratio
when its input and output rates are equal.
Signed PCM spans -32768 through 32767. Native floating-point workspaces retain
PCM amplitude units. FFmpeg format conversion supplies the graph's normalized
audio representation. dBFS measurements use the full signed PCM range.

The app_rpt-to-hardware-callback program ring is the only cross-clock audio
queue between app_rpt and the native path. It accepts PCM at the active app_rpt
rate and renders from its first available sample; it has no source-audio startup
fill or playback reserve. Its 110 ms occupancy target is a clock-recovery
setpoint, not a fixed latency or keyed-transmit admission delay. One persistent
libsamplerate stream converts the source rate to the native rate and gently
adjusts its ratio for independent clock drift, including when the nominal ratio
is one-to-one. This correction is independent of device and network jitter
buffers and changes no PTT state. A temporary program-source shortfall is
concealed smoothly by the callback renderer rather than rendered as an abrupt
silence insertion.

Both adapters admit app_rpt program frames continuously, including idle
silence. A native-parrot implementation may be the sole external program-audio
producer only while it supplies native-rate samples through that same lock-free
program ring. Legacy app_rpt echo is distinct and is not an external producer
under this exception. PTT transitions preserve program-ring state. Native-rate
input uses the same stream with a nominal one-to-one ratio. This continuous PCM
flow affects audio only: program-ring occupancy neither requests nor releases
PTT. The signaling engine retains ownership of the desired PTT state.

The hardware callback directly renders each prepared native block. It owns
local receive processing, final voice/telemetry processing, CTCSS/DCS
generation, and native DAC PCM rendering. The program ring is its only
program-audio bridge: app_rpt normally produces it, and native-parrot playback
may temporarily supply native-rate samples through it. No other audio worker
is permitted; there is no per-channel native worker thread or native
input/output worker queue. A program-source shortfall is concealed in the
callback without changing PTT, COS, CTCSS, or DCS state.

The device writer submits one 48 kHz stereo block per completed capture
interval, including while idle. COS and CTCSS decode do not control device
write cadence. The OSS writer does not prepend silence when its queue empties.
Its queue-depth bound and short/failed-write diagnostics still apply during
idle operation. After a complete non-silent DAC submission, the adapter holds
physical `txPttOut` through the measured or estimated device playout delay plus
one native 20 ms block. It submits silence while draining, cancels the hold on
a new key request, and never advances the hold for a short, failed, or
underflowed submission. OSS measures queue delay after the write with an
available-space fallback; PortAudio uses the output latency reported when the
stream opens. This is a best-effort queue-drain guard, not a second PTT owner.
PortAudio otherwise retains the ASL audio API's device recovery.

When the local chain is enabled, its enabled RNNoise and FFmpeg dynamics
stages advance in the direct callback every native interval with
receive cpu_saver_enabled=no. RX CPU saver bypasses those stages while the
receiver is unqualified. Existing receive qualification, repeat routing, and
final transmit muting still control audibility; processing continuity does not
key the transmitter.

Hardware mixer settings use 0 through 999. Hardware gain is expressed in dB
relative to setting 500. Receiver CTCSS decoder gain affects the detector input;
it does not change voice gain. Native CTCSS is generated at the direct
`[ctcss] transmit_peak_dbfs` PCM peak and mixed after transmitter dynamics and
limiting. Hardware output gain then sets the DAC's analog level. Saturating PCM
conversion prevents signed wraparound.
The transmit preemphasis_enabled option controls only the composite graph's
pre-emphasis stage. Final limiting uses the voice/telemetry profile's explicit
lookahead-limiter controls independently of pre-emphasis.

Native DCS uses its own direct `[dcs] peak_dbfs` PCM peak and a fixed shared-FFmpeg
spectral-shaping graph. It bypasses CTCSS generation, transmitter pre-emphasis,
and speech processing, while sharing only the configured hardware output route
and downstream hardware gain.

Each direction declares exactly one signaling method in its `[receive]` or
`[transmit]` profile. Selecting receive CTCSS requires a non-disabled CTCSS
source and a supported receive-tone list; selecting transmit CTCSS requires
one supported default transmit tone. A receive-to-transmit map is required
only when both directions select CTCSS; then both supported-tone lists must
have equal length. Selecting DCS requires the corresponding DCS code in
`000N`- through `777I`-style spelling
(three octal digits followed by `N` or `I`). The
configuration parser rejects
an incomplete or invalid selected method before a channel can start, rather
than allowing the radio engine to silently fall back to carrier behavior.
For a configured profile, omitted clean-slate `[receive]`, `[transmit]`,
`[ctcss]`, and `[dcs]` options resolve to the concrete values in the shipped
sample. Explicit scoped values take precedence over flat-section values.
Integer signaling controls validate the exact storage units used by the channel
adapter: receive squelch delay is 0--511 ms, receive-on and transmit-off delay
are 0--3000 20 ms frames, receive CTCSS relax is 0 or 1, and transmit/receive
frequencies are nonnegative integral hertz. This keeps a successful reload
from deferring a malformed numeric value to channel construction.

@section concurrency Thread and resource ownership

Profile snapshot getters clear their output when a radio profile is absent.
Render paths must never consume uninitialized gain, routing, or CTCSS data
while a profile is unavailable; the missing-profile status remains available to
callers.

Each channel owns its native graphs, detector, program ring, and a
callback-owned native renderer. Setup preallocates the renderer's resamplers,
RNNoise state, echo recording, workspaces, and diagnostics. The hardware
callback owns mutable per-frame renderer state, consumes the program ring, and
directly produces the DAC block. It has no native worker to start or join and
no native input/output queue to drain. The processing settings mutex protects
profile snapshots. Never hold it while looking up or locking an Asterisk
channel: app_rpt can read processing settings while holding channel locks. The
scanner copies the needed profile data before channel lookup.

Native-parrot playback may be a separate producer only when it hands
native-rate samples to the hardware callback through the lock-free program
ring. Legacy app_rpt echo is not covered by this exception. No other audio
worker is permitted.

The native radio callback, including carrier and CTCSS detector stages, uses
only preallocated caller-owned state and prepared graphs. It must not call
Asterisk logging, take locks, allocate memory, or perform file I/O. It directly
executes the prepared FFmpeg graphs, RNNoise, and persistent sample-rate
conversion when those stages apply; graph construction, reconfiguration, and
destruction remain outside the callback. PTT is requested synchronously from
the current signaling-engine state at the callback boundary. A completed
non-silent DAC submission may hold physical `txPttOut` for the adapter's
playout-delay guard while the callback feeds silence. Program-ring occupancy
and audio shortfalls must never key or unkey the transmitter.

Native renderer measurements are published as coherent lock-free snapshots.
Control-plane meters and reset requests must use that snapshot/request boundary
instead of reading or changing callback-owned graph, RNNoise, SRC, or echo state
directly. A reader receives the most recently published complete snapshot, or
reports it unavailable after bounded retries rather than waiting for rendering;
a pinned pair of snapshot buffers can therefore leave measurements briefly stale.

Configured radio traces remain preallocated in-memory diagnostic buffers. The
obsolete receive/transmit capture toggles and file-capture path are absent, so
they cannot add callback I/O or an alternate real-time path.

Each incoming-link audiohook owns a prepared FFmpeg graph, a preallocated
conversion workspace, and a reader-pinned statistics snapshot through its
Asterisk datastore. The callback synchronously processes the current eligible
frame in place. It has no deferred link-rendering thread or input/output SPSC
handoff queues. Graph preparation and replacement occur outside the callback;
the callback uses an atomically published prepared graph and never rebuilds it.

A format or capacity mismatch, an unavailable graph, or a graph-processing
error leaves the current Asterisk frame unchanged rather than allocating,
reconfiguring, delaying, or replaying it. The prepared FFmpeg graph's bounded
internal FIFO can report startup fill or runtime shortfall without creating a
separate callback handoff. The published callback snapshot records those graph
conditions, synchronous processing errors, and input/output levels. CLI
readers pin a published snapshot buffer and can receive a briefly stale or
unavailable snapshot rather than waiting for the callback. Link processing
preserves the attached channel's negotiated sample rate and has no PTT policy.

Hook teardown detaches and destroys the audiohook, quiescing its synchronous
callback before its graph slot or callback-owned workspace is released. A
reload atomically publishes a complete prepared graph before changing link
admission, so each callback uses either the old graph, the new graph, or a
clean bypass. Hardware adapters own their device references and coordinate
device and HID state when swapping USB assignments.

@section verification Building and checking the reference

Run `make docs` with Doxygen 1.9.8 or newer, Graphviz, and Python 3 installed.
Generated HTML is written to `build/doxygen/html`; XML is written beside it. Doxygen checks
undocumented symbols and enum values, parameter and return documentation, and
invalid markup. Every diagnostic fails the target.

The required quality workflow runs Doxygen concurrently with lint and static
analysis. All three results must succeed before the Debian 12/13 amd64/arm64
test matrix. Release callers depend on that same required quality workflow.
Only successful main-branch checks publish this reference to GitHub Pages.

Shell documentation uses double-hash comments. The documentation filter exposes
actual shell function names and those comments to Doxygen without executing
shell code or supplying missing documentation. Python uses standard docstrings
with Doxygen commands for parameters and return values.
*/
